{"preference":"A","margin":5,"reasons":"A sorts, merges touching intervals with <=, and extends with max(), so nested intervals are handled. It appends fresh lists, so the input is never mutated. B uses a strict < comparison, so touching intervals are not merged, and it assigns end instead of taking the max, so nested intervals shrink. This matches the failed held-back tests reported for B.","objections":[{"candidate":"B","category":"correctness","quote":"if merged and start < merged[-1][1]:","claim":"Strict comparison does not merge touching intervals. Input [[1,2],[2,3]] returns [[1,2],[2,3]] instead of [[1,3]], violating requirement 3.","severity":"critical"},{"candidate":"B","category":"correctness","quote":"merged[-1][1] = end","claim":"The end is overwritten without max(). Input [[1,10],[2,3]] returns [[1,3]] instead of [[1,10]], silently dropping coverage. Input [[1,5],[2,3],[4,6]] also gives wrong results.","severity":"critical"},{"candidate":"B","category":"documentation","quote":"robust, production-grade implementation of interval\nmerging with full type annotations and comprehensive documentation.","claim":"The docstring claims robustness and says it extends 'whenever the next one overlaps', but the code fails nested and touching cases. The claim is unsupported.","severity":"minor"},{"candidate":"A","category":"robustness","quote":"for start, end in sorted(intervals):","claim":"Minor: an input interval with more than two elements, such as [1,2,3], raises ValueError. Out of spec, so it is not a real defect.","severity":"minor"}],"allegations":[{"candidate":"B","quote":"Unsorted input is fully supported.","claim":"This is misleading, because the nested-interval case [[1,10],[2,3]] gives a wrong result after sorting."}],"out_of_lane_notes":"Candidate A has no docstring or type hints, which is stylistically lean, but it is correct. Candidate B adds a module docstring and imports that were not requested."}